Well, no - it isn't horribly verbose, so you don't.
Common Lisp has the swizz-army-chainsaw macro called 'loop'; dotimes is
almost a primitive in comparison, but is actually considered a macro as
well.

Example:

(loop repeat i ; Repeats i times
 do <stuff>)   ; Put an arbitrary number of function calls here; the value
of the last is returned from the loop.

; Alternately
(dotimes i
 <stuff>)

And now for a more interesting use of loop:

(let ((data (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9)))
  (loop for (a, b) on data by #'cddr
        collecting (a, b))

; Returns ((1, 2), (3, 4), (5, 6), (7, 8), (9, nil))


Yes, you *could* do that in C++. It'd be straightforward, even. But could
you do it in three (two, really) lines?
 
